// VMFL045 geometry, frozen in PREREGISTRATION.md section 4.  Single trapezoidal
// block; the whole bottom edge is the 15 deg ramp.
//
//   corner (ramp apex) at (0, 0)  -- the compression corner; the wall deflects
//                                    the flow up by 15 deg, forming an attached
//                                    weak oblique shock from this point
//   inlet plane   x = 0            (left, x = 0, height 0 -> 0.5)
//   outlet plane  x = 0.6 m        (right, supersonic, zeroGradient)
//   top           y = 0.5 m        (horizontal)
//   ramp / wall   y = x*tan(15 deg),  x in [0, 0.6]
//                 tan(15 deg) = 0.2679491924311227
//                 y(x=0.6)    = 0.16076951545867362
//   2-D slab      z in [-0.01, +0.01], one cell, `empty`
//
// GEOMETRY IS RECONSTRUCTED, NOT COPIED (PREREGISTRATION.md section 1a): the
// manual gives the ramp angle (15 deg), the ramp length (0.4572 m) and the
// sample point (0.38, 0.14) but NOT the domain extent, inlet-to-corner distance
// or top height (its flow-domain figure is not in the text sidecar).  This
// domain is chosen so that (a) the sample point (0.38, 0.14) lies in the uniform
// post-shock region, (b) the frozen sampling zone (section 5) is > 6 coarse
// cells clear of both the shock and the wall, and (c) the shock LEAVES through
// the supersonic outlet and never reaches the top -- no wave is reflected.
//
// WHY THE TOP IS AT 0.5 AND THE OUTLET AT 0.6: the oblique shock leaves the
// corner at beta = 36.92317760072534 deg (the frozen weak-shock angle for the
// as-modelled M1 = 2.501814636762496).  At the outlet x = 0.6 the shock stands
// at y = 0.6*tan(beta) = 0.4508724 < 0.5, so the ENTIRE shock leaves through the
// outlet; it would reach the top only at x = 0.5/tan(beta) = 0.6654 > 0.6.  The
// top boundary sees undisturbed M = 2.5018 freestream along its whole length and
// cannot reflect anything, because nothing arrives at it.
//
// Two cell counts are substituted by run_vmfl045.sh from the frozen level table:
// cells along x (__NX__) and cells across the block height (__NY__).  Every
// level DOUBLES both:
//   L1  90 x  76  ->   6840 cells,  h_x = 0.006667
//   L2 180 x 152  ->  27360 cells,  h_x = 0.003333
//   L3 360 x 304  -> 109440 cells,  h_x = 0.001667
// so h halves EXACTLY and the Roache refinement ratio is r = 2 BY CONSTRUCTION,
// never inferred from a cell count.

scale   1;

vertices
(
    ( 0.0   0.0                  -0.01)   //  0
    ( 0.6   0.16076951545867362  -0.01)   //  1
    ( 0.6   0.5                  -0.01)   //  2
    ( 0.0   0.5                  -0.01)   //  3
    ( 0.0   0.0                   0.01)   //  4
    ( 0.6   0.16076951545867362   0.01)   //  5
    ( 0.6   0.5                   0.01)   //  6
    ( 0.0   0.5                   0.01)   //  7
);

blocks
(
    hex (0 1 2 3 4 5 6 7) (__NX__ __NY__ 1) simpleGrading (1 1 1)
);

edges
(
);

